  "question": {
    "text": "A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> long wire of resistance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>ohm</mi></math> and diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> is coated with plastic insulation of thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>06</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. When a current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo></math>ampere flows through the wire, find the temperature difference across the insulation in steady-state if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>×</mo><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>cal</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>cm</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.",
